<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colorado</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colorado is a state of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, crossed by the Rocky Mountains and the high plains to their east. Its eastern plains were part of the hunting lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> and Arapaho before American settlement. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pike</a>'s Peak gold rush of 1858–1859 brought miners and towns such as Denver, and Colorado became the 38th state in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colorado.jpg" alt="Colorado" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Andrew Carlyle Carson Vector: Fleminra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colorado.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Colorado is a landlocked state in the Mountain West. It shares the Four Corners region with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, and is bordered by Wyoming to the north, Nebraska to the northeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> to the southeast. It takes in most of the Southern Rocky Mountains, the northeastern portion of the Colorado Plateau and the western edge of the Great Plains, and its landscape includes alpine mountains, high plains, deserts with huge sand dunes and deep canyons.</p><p>In 1861 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> defined the boundaries of the new Territory of Colorado exclusively by lines of latitude and longitude, from 37°N to 41°N and from 102°02′48″W to 109°02′48″W. After 165 years of government surveys the borders were officially defined by 697 boundary markers and 697 straight lines. Colorado, Wyoming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> are the only states whose borders are defined solely by straight lines with no natural features. The Four Corners Monument is the only place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> where four states meet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region has been inhabited by Native Americans and their Paleo-Indian ancestors for at least 13,500 years and possibly more than 37,000. The Lindenmeier site contains artifacts dating from approximately 8720 BCE. The Ancient Pueblo peoples lived in the valleys and mesas of the Colorado Plateau in the far southwest, and the Ute Nation inhabited the mountain valleys of the Rocky Mountains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> lived in the east and southeast. In the 17th century the Arapaho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> moved west from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region to hunt across the High Plains.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> acquired the territorial claim to the eastern Rocky Mountains with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1803. In 1806 Zebulon Pike led an army reconnaissance expedition into the disputed region. Spanish cavalry arrested him and his troops in the San Luis Valley the following February, took them to Chihuahua and expelled them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in July.</p><p>In 1848 much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Nuevo México</a> region was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pike</a>'s Peak Gold Rush of 1858-1862 brought an influx of settlers. On 28 February 1861 President James Buchanan signed the act creating the Territory of Colorado, and on 1 August 1876 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> signed Proclamation 230, admitting Colorado as the 38th state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Denver is the capital, the most populous city and the centre of the Front Range Urban Corridor. Colorado Springs is the second-most populous city. The state is the eighth-largest by area. Its economy rests on government and defence, mining, agriculture, tourism and manufacturing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colorado” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1779 Governor Juan Bautista de Anza of New Mexico, with Ute, Apache and Pueblo auxiliaries, surprised and killed the war leader Cuerno Verde in present-day southern <b>Colorado</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>One hypothesis brings them from the Great Plains and links them with the Dismal River culture, dated 1675–1725 and excavated in Nebraska, eastern <b>Colorado</b> and western Kansas.</blockquote><a href="/culture/apache">Apache · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dent site in <b>Colorado</b> gave the first known association of Clovis points with mammoth bones.</blockquote><a href="/culture/clovis-culture">Clovis culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1893 Katharine Lee Bates, a 33-year-old English professor at Wellesley College, took the train to <b>Colorado</b> Springs to teach at Colorado College.</blockquote><a href="/songs/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a></li><li><blockquote>…Weatherly never set the poem to any tune himself: Jess, who lived with her husband Edward Weatherly at the Neosho mine near Ouray, <b>Colorado</b>, set it to the air in 1913, having heard it as a child in California, played by her father and other Irish railway workers.</blockquote><a href="/songs/danny-boy">Danny Boy</a></li><li><blockquote>Investigating on behalf of the Music Publishers Protection Association, Samuel Moanfeldt also found a similar &quot;<b>Colorado</b> Home&quot;, but within a few months concluded that Higley had written the poem and Kelley had set it to music.</blockquote><a href="/songs/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a></li></ul></section>
